    <!-- <p> In <xref ref="ex-continuum-assumption" />, you will consider whether salt flow can be -->
    <!--   considered under a continuum assumption. </p> -->
    <!---->
    <!-- <figure xml:id="fig-salt-flow"> -->
    <!--   <caption>An AI-generated image of salt flowing out of a large orifice.</caption> -->
    <!--   <image source="saltflow.png" width="40%"> -->
    <!--     <shortdescription>Salt flow</shortdescription> -->
    <!--   </image> -->
    <!-- </figure> -->

    <p> The derivation of the governing equations for a fluid is then based on three basic
      principles of physics: <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Conservation of mass:</em> mass is neither created nor destroyed. </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Conservation of momentum:</em> the rate of change of momentum of a portion of the
      fluid is equal to the applied force (Newton's second law). </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Conservation of energy:</em> energy is neither created nor destroyed. (We will not
            need energy conservation in this course.) </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p> Before starting, though, our first task is to derive some helpful results on the
      differentiation of quantities such as <xref ref="eqn-mass" />. </p>

  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-RTT">
    <title>Reynolds' Transport Theorem</title>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-jacob">
      <title>Jacobian of the Lagrangian-to-Eulerian map</title>

      <p> Consider a volume of fluid that, at time <m>t = 0</m>, occupies the region <m>V(0)
        \subseteq D</m> and is dyed a certain colour. As the fluid evolves, this packet of fluid
        occupies the region <m>V(t)</m> for <m>t > 0</m>. </p>

      <p> As an example, we may express the density of the fluid as <me>
          \rho = \rho(\bx, t), \qquad \textrm{$\bx \in V(t)$}
        </me> i.e. the density is given as a function of position in space and time, for points
        in the (possibly moving) region <m>V(t)</m>. This is the natural description for an
        observer at a fixed point in space, and is the <em>Eulerian</em> viewpoint. </p>

      <p> Alternatively, we can write <me>
          \rho = \rho(\bX, t), \qquad \textrm{$\bX \in V(0)$}
        </me> which is the
        density of fluid for those particles making up an originally chosen volume, <m>V(0)</m>.
        This is a natural quantity to study if we were to move with the fluid; for instance, if we
        were to colour the volume <m>V(0)</m> with a dye and track its density in time. This is
        the <em>Lagrangian</em> viewpoint. </p>

      <p> Strictly, <m>\rho(\bX, t)</m> and <m>\rho(\bx, t)</m> are different functions of their
        arguments; we follow the common convention of using the same symbol for both, and the
        arguments make clear which is meant. </p>

      <p> The correspondence between the Eulerian and Lagrangian coordinates is written as <me>
          \bx = \bx(\bX, t).
        </me>
      </p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-jacobian">
        <title>Jacobian of the Lagrangian-to-Eulerian map</title>
        <statement>
          <p>Since we assume the medium is continuous, we would generally require that the
            mapping from Lagrangian to Eulerian coordinates is continuous and one-to-one; then the
            map assigns every element (label) in the original reference configuration, denoted <m>
            \bX</m>, a unique position, <m>\bx</m>, in the deformed state.</p>

          <p>From Multivariable Calculus, the map is locally invertible provided the <em>Jacobian
            of the transformation</em>, <me>
              J = \pd{\bx}{\bX} = \pd{(x, y, z)}{(X, Y, Z)} =
              \begin{vmatrix}
              \pd{x}{X} &amp; \pd{x}{Y} &amp; \pd{x}{Z} \\
              \pd{y}{X} &amp; \pd{y}{Y} &amp; \pd{y}{Z} \\
              \pd{z}{X} &amp; \pd{z}{Y} &amp; \pd{z}{Z}
              \end{vmatrix}
            </me>
            is finite and non-zero. Since <m>J = 1</m> at <m>t = 0</m> (when <m>\bx = \bX</m>) and
            <m>J</m> varies continuously in time, this means <m>J > 0</m>, i.e. <me>
              0 \lt J \lt \infty.
            </me>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>

      <p> The following requires a bit of algebra, and you are not required to prove the result.</p>
      <theorem xml:id="thm-Euleridentity">
        <title>Euler's identity</title>
        <statement>
          <p> The material derivative of the Jacobian of the transformation <m>\bx = \bx(\bX, t)</m>
            is given by <men xml:id="eqn-euler-identity">
              \DD{J}{t} = J \nabla \cdot \bu,
            </men> where <m>\bu =
            \DD{\bx}{t}</m>. </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
          <p> (Not examinable) The material derivative is the time derivative with <m>\bX</m> held fixed, so
            <m>\pd{x}{t} = u</m>, <m>\pd{y}{t} = v</m> and <m>\pd{z}{t} = w</m>. By the product
            rule, <m>\DD{J}{t}</m> is a sum of three determinants, each with one row of <m>J</m>
            differentiated. </p>

          <p> Consider the first. A typical entry of its differentiated row is <me>
              \DD{}{t}\pd{x}{X} = \pd{u}{X} = \pd{u}{x}\pd{x}{X} + \pd{u}{y}\pd{y}{X} +
              \pd{u}{z}\pd{z}{X},
            </me> using the chain rule; the entries for the <m>Y</m> and <m>Z</m> columns are the
            same, with <m>X</m> replaced by <m>Y</m> and <m>Z</m> respectively. So the new first row is <me>
              \pd{u}{x}\,(\text{row 1}) + \pd{u}{y}\,(\text{row 2}) + \pd{u}{z}\,(\text{row 3}).
            </me> A determinant with two equal rows vanishes, so only the first term survives, and
            this determinant equals <m>\pd{u}{x}J</m>. </p>

          <p> In the same way, the other two determinants give <m>\pd{v}{y}J</m> and
            <m>\pd{w}{z}J</m>. Adding them gives <m>\DD{J}{t} = J\,\nabla\cdot\bu</m>. </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-RTT">
      <title>Reynolds' Transport Theorem</title>

      <p> We are now ready to derive a key result that eases our path towards developing the
        governing equations for a fluid. The result is as follows.</p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-RTT">
        <title>Reynolds' Transport Theorem</title>
        <statement>
          <p> Consider a time-dependent volume, <m>V(t)</m>, that is transported by the fluid so
            that it always consists of the same fluid particles. Then, for any function, <m>f(\bx,
              t)</m>, continuously differentiable with respect to its arguments, <em>Reynolds'
            Transport Theorem</em> is as follows: <men xml:id="eqn-RTT">
              \dd{}{t} \iiint_{V(t)} f \, \de{x} \, \de{y} \, \de{z} = \iiint_{V(t)} \left[
              \pd{f}{t} + \nabla \cdot (f\bu)\right] \, \de{x} \, \de{y} \, \de{z}.
            </men>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
          <p> We transform the integral in Eulerian coordinates to Lagrangian coordinates,
            integrating in the label space: <me>
              I(t) \equiv \iiint_{V(t)} f \, \de{x} \de{y} \de{z}
              = \iiint_{V(0)} f J \, \de{X} \de{Y} \de{Z},
            </me>
            and notice that we now only need to integrate over the fixed volume as defined in
            Lagrangian space, at the expense of adding the Jacobian factor. We now write <md>
              <mrow> \dd{I}{t} &amp;= \iiint_{V(0)} \DD{(fJ)}{t} \, \de{X}\de{Y}\de{Z} </mrow>
            </md>
            and the material derivative passes through since the domain is fixed. The material derivative theorem, <xref
              ref="thm-material-derivative" />, now allows us to convert the material derivative to
            regular partial derivatives. By the product rule: <md>
              <mrow>\DD{(fJ)}{t} &amp;= \DD{f}{t}J + f\DD{J}{t} </mrow>
              <mrow> &amp;= \left[ \pd{f}{t} + \bu \cdot \nabla f\right]J
                + f \left[J \nabla \cdot \bu\right] </mrow>
              <mrow> &amp;= \left[ \pd{f}{t} + \nabla \cdot (f\bu) \right]J </mrow>
            </md>
            and we have differentiated the Jacobian via Euler's identity <xref
              ref="thm-Euleridentity" /> in the second line. The last line follows from the vector
            identity: <me>
              \nabla \cdot (f\bu) = f \nabla \cdot \bu + \nabla f \cdot \bu.
            </me>
            We can now revert from Lagrangian to Eulerian integration, and this completes the
            proof of Reynolds' Transport Theorem.</p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <note>
        <p>It is helpful for you to convince yourself that the vector identity used in the proof is
          the only possible arrangement of operations that makes sense, i.e. in order for <m>\nabla
          \cdot
            (f\bu)</m> to return a scalar.</p>
      </note>

      <p>In summary, Reynolds' Transport Theorem gives an identity for how time
        differentiation can
        pass through the integral when the domain of integration is changing in time!</p>

      <example xml:id="ex-rtt-stretching-cube-example">
        <title>Reynolds' Transport Theorem for a stretching cube</title>

        <statement>
          <aside>
            <title>New in 2026</title>
            <p>This worked example is a new addition to the notes in 2026.</p>
          </aside>

          <p> Consider the steady velocity field <me>
              \bu = \left(x, \; -\tfrac12 y, \; 0\right).
            </me> At <m>t = 0</m> the fluid occupying the unit cube <m>V(0) = [0,1]^3</m> is
            dyed, and <m>V(t)</m> denotes the material volume occupied by this dyed fluid at later
            times. Let <me>
              f(\bx) = x^2 + y, \qquad I(t) = \iiint_{V(t)} f \, \de{x} \, \de{y} \, \de{z}.
            </me>
          </p>

          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
              <p> Find the pathlines <m>\bx = \bx(\bX, t)</m> and hence show that <m>V(t)</m>
                remains a cuboid. Find its dimensions. </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p> Compute the Jacobian <m>J = \pd{(x, y, z)}{(X, Y, Z)}</m> and verify Euler's
                identity <xref ref="eqn-euler-identity" />. Is the flow incompressible? </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>

        <hint>
          <p> The natural next step is to compute <m>I(t)</m> and <m>\de{I}/\de{t}</m>. Since <m>
            V(t)</m> is a box with known dimensions, <m>I(t)</m> can be found by integrating <m>f</m>
            directly over <m>V(t)</m>. Alternatively, map back to the fixed cube <m>V(0)</m> using
            the Jacobian from part (b), exactly as in the proof of <xref ref="thm-RTT" />. To verify
            Reynolds' Transport Theorem, compute <m>\nabla \cdot (f\bu)</m> (note that <m>\pd{f}{t}
            = 0</m>) and integrate it over <m>V(t)</m>; the two answers should agree. You will carry
            this out in <xref ref="ex-rtt-stretching-cube" />. </p>
        </hint>

        <solution>
          <p>
            <alert>(a)</alert> The pathlines satisfy <m>\dot{x} = x</m>, <m>\dot{y} = -y/2</m>, <m>
            \dot{z} = 0</m> with <m>(x, y, z) = (X, Y, Z)</m> at <m>t = 0</m>, so <me>
              x = X\e^{t}, \qquad y = Y\e^{-t/2}, \qquad z = Z.
            </me> Each coordinate is
            mapped independently of the others, so the faces of the cube remain planes and <me>
              V(t) = [0, \, \e^{t}] \times [0, \, \e^{-t/2}] \times [0, \, 1].
            </me> The cube is
            stretched in <m>x</m> and squashed in <m>y</m>, as shown in <xref
              ref="fig-rtt-stretching-cube" />. </p>

          <figure xml:id="fig-rtt-stretching-cube">
            <caption>The material volume <m>V(t)</m> at <m>t = 0, 0.5, 1</m>; the dotted line is
              the pathline of the corner initially at <m>(1, 1, 0)</m>.</caption>
            <image source="ch-chapter03-rtt-cube.png" width="80%">
              <shortdescription>Three overlapping rectangles showing the dyed volume stretching in
                x and shrinking in y over time.</shortdescription>
            </image>
          </figure>

          <p>
            <alert>(b)</alert> The Jacobian matrix is diagonal, so <me>
              J = \begin{vmatrix}
              \e^{t} &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\
              0 &amp; \e^{-t/2} &amp; 0 \\
              0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1
              \end{vmatrix} = \e^{t/2}.
            </me> Since <m>J</m> does not
            depend on <m>\bX</m>, we have <m>\DD{J}{t} = \dd{J}{t} = \tfrac12 \e^{t/2}</m>.
            Meanwhile, <m>\nabla \cdot \bu = 1 - \tfrac12 = \tfrac12</m>, so <me>
              J \nabla \cdot \bu = \tfrac12 \e^{t/2} = \DD{J}{t},
            </me> as required by
            Euler's identity <xref ref="thm-Euleridentity" />. The flow is <em>not</em>
            incompressible: the volume of the box is <m>|V(t)| = J|V(0)| = \e^{t/2}</m>, which grows in
            time. </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <remark>
        <title>A 1D version of Reynolds' Transport Theorem</title>
        <p> In 1D, Reynolds' Transport Theorem reduces to an identity known as Leibniz's rule. This
          is presented as an exercise in <xref ref="ex-hw-leibnitz-rule" />. </p>
      </remark>

      <remark>
        <title>RTT and conserved quantities</title>
        <p> Reynolds' Transport Theorem is particularly useful when the integral quantity <me>
            B = \iiint_{V(t)} f \, \de{x} \, \de{y} \, \de{z}
          </me> is a <em>conserved
          quantity</em>. A conserved quantity is something for which we can apply a <em>principle of
          conservation</em>. In practice for this course, this is usually mass or linear momentum: <ul>
            <li>
              <p> Mass: <m>f=\rho</m> and the left-hand side of <xref ref="eqn-RTT" /> is zero
          because mass is conserved. </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p> Linear momentum: <m>\bf=\rho\bu</m> is now a vector quantity and, by <em>Newton's
          second law</em>, the left-hand side of <xref ref="eqn-RTT" /> equals the sum of all
          the forces acting on the fluid in the material volume <m>V(t)</m>, which are: <ul>
                  <li>
                    <p> Surface forces: Forces acting on the surfaces of the fluid, which could be
          reaction forces from the walls of a container, or forces due to pressure or stress from
          surrounding fluid. (Note that we will cover stress in <xref ref="ch-viscousflows" />.) </p>
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <p>
                      Body forces: Forces acting over the interior of the fluid,
                      and in this course you will usually only need to consider the force
                      of gravity, although in principle other body forces could act
                      such as an electromagnetic force. In addition, if working in
                      a non-inertial frame of reference, other apparent forces need to
                      be added (forces due to linear acceleration, angular acceleration
                      and the Coriolis and centrifugal forces).
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </p>
            </li>
            <!--              <li>
                <p>
                  Angular momentum: In this case, the angular momentum per unit volume is
                  <m>\bf=\rho\br\times\bu</m> and the left-hand side of <xref ref="eqn-RTT"/>
                  is the torque applied to the system of particles in the fluid.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Energy: Energy can be classified as internal, kinetic, gravitational potential, etc.
                </p>
              </li>-->
          </ul>
        </p>
      </remark>


      <!-- Remark on the general (control-volume) statement of RTT removed from the notes in 2026.
      <remark xml:id="rem-RTT-control-volume">
        <title>General statement of RTT</title>
        <p>
          <em>Non-examinable:</em>
        </p>

        <p>A more general statement of Reynolds' Transport Theorem is as follows: we define a <em>control
          volume</em> <m>V(t)</m>. This is a region of the fluid that we specify; it could be any
          fluid-containing volume, and in particular it doesn't need to follow the fluid particles
          (unlike <xref ref="eqn-RTT" />). Then the rate of change of <m>B</m> in the system of
          particles equals the integral of the rate of change of <m>f</m> over the control volume
          plus the net flux of <m>f</m> through the surface of the control volume: <men
            xml:id="eqn-RTT-simp">
            \dd{B_{\textrm{syst}}}{t} = \dd{B_{CV}}{t}+\dot{B}_{CS}.
          </men>
          Here, <m>B_{\textrm{syst}}</m> is the amount of <m>B</m> in the system of particles (so its
          time derivative is the Lagrangian, or material, rate of change of <m>B</m>), <m>B_{CV}</m> is the amount of <m>B</m> in the
          control volume and <m>\dot{B}_{CS}</m> is the flux of <m>B</m> across the surface of the
          control volume. Note that, if <m>B</m> is conserved then we can write the left-hand side
          in terms of other things (zero if <m>B</m> is mass and the sum of the forces if <m>B</m>
          is linear momentum). </p>
        <p> This is in a form that can be used to solve many problems in engineering. The key point
          is that these problems are about quantities on the scale of the <em>control volume</em>
          and not about the details of the flow at particular points. </p>
        <p>
          Thus, for example we could consider a tank with inlets and outlets and,
          using the fluid in the tank as the control volume, we can apply the conservation
          of mass to find the volume flow rates in the inlets and outlets and the level of
          fluid in the tank.
        </p>
        <p>
          We can use momentum conservation to find the force on a section of a pipe
          or the dynamics of a piston moving a fluid to which a known force is applied.
        </p>
      </remark> -->
      <!--      <p>
        You will apply Reynolds' Transport Theorem to a problem involving the conservation
        of mass in <xref ref="ex-rtt-mass-tank"/> and problems on momentum conservation in
        <xref ref="ex-rtt-momentum-artery"/> and <xref ref="ex-rtt-momentum-pipe"/>
      </p>-->

    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section>
    <title>Conservation of mass</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Our task in this section is to prove the following equation for the conservation of mass
        of a fluid:</p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-mass">
        <title>Continuity equation</title>
        <statement>
          <p> The differential form of the law of conservation of mass, otherwise known as the <em>continuity
            equation</em>, is <men xml:id="eqn-consvmass1">
              \pd{\rho}{t} + \nabla \cdot (\rho \bu) = 0,
            </men> where <m>\rho
            = \rho(\bx,t)</m> is the density of the fluid and <m>\bu = \bu(\bx, t)</m> is the
            velocity of the fluid. </p>

          <p> The above form is equivalent, on expanding <m>\nabla \cdot (\rho \bu)</m> and using the
            definition of the material derivative, to <men
              xml:id="eqn-consvmass2">
              \DD{\rho}{t} + \rho(\nabla \cdot \bu) = 0.
            </men>

          </p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>

      <p> In fact, as it turns out, the proof of this result is trivial if we use Reynolds'
        Transport Theorem and Lagrangian formulation following <xref ref="thm-RTT" />.</p>

      <proof>
        <p> Consider the mass contained in an arbitrary material volume <m>V(t)</m>, <me>
            \iiint_{V(t)} \rho \, \de{V}.
          </me> Since <m>V(t)</m> always consists of the same fluid particles, conservation of
          mass requires that this mass does not change in time, i.e. its time derivative is
          zero. </p>

        <p> By Reynolds' Transport Theorem (<xref ref="thm-RTT" />), we can pass the time
          derivative through the integral. This gives <me>
            0 = \dd{}{t}\iiint_{V(t)} \rho \, \de{V} = \iiint_{V(t)} \left[ \pd{\rho}{t} + \nabla
          \cdot (\rho \bu)\right] \, \de{V}.
          </me>
          Since this holds for every material volume <m>V(t) \subseteq D</m>, the bump lemma
          (<xref ref="lem-bump" />) implies that the integrand must be zero. This gives <xref
            ref="eqn-consvmass1" />. </p>
        <p> The proof of <xref ref="eqn-consvmass2" /> follows immediately from application of the
          definition of the material derivative <xref ref="eqn-DDt" />. </p>
      </proof>

      <p>
        Within the above proof, we use an idea used throughout this chapter,
        which is that if an integral of a quantity (the integrand) is zero for all possible
        domains of integration, then the integrand, itself, is zero.
        This is sometimes referred to as the "Bump lemma".
      </p>

      <lemma xml:id="lem-bump">
        <title>Bump lemma</title>
        <statement>
          <p> Let <m>f(\bx)</m> be a sufficiently smooth function defined on <m>\Omega \subseteq
            \mathbb{R}^n</m>. Suppose it is the case that <me>
              \int_V f(\bx) \, \de{V} = 0,
            </me> for all <m>V \subseteq
            \Omega</m>. Then <me>
              f(\bx) \equiv 0
            </me> in <m>\Omega</m>. </p>
        </statement>
      </lemma>

      <p> In <xref ref="ex-bump" />, you will prove the above lemma. </p>
    </introduction>


    <subsection>
      <title>Derivation of mass conservation using Eulerian methods</title>

      <p> The derivation we have just shown for <xref ref="thm-mass" />, using the Lagrangian
        viewpoint and Reynolds' Transport Theorem, is deceptively simple, and it is
        instructive to see how the result is proved purely from the perspective of Eulerian
        coordinates. </p>

      <p> For this, let us consider <m>V</m> to be a <em>fixed and closed</em> subregion of the
        overall fluid, <m>D</m>, that does not change with time. An illustration of this is shown in <xref
          ref="fig-normalvolume" />.</p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-normalvolume">
        <caption>The fixed fluid volume, <m>V</m> (blue), with boundary <m>\partial V</m>. On a small
          patch of the boundary, the velocity <m>\bu</m> has normal component <m>\bu \cdot
          \bn</m>, where <m>\bn</m> is the outward unit normal; this gives the outward
          flux.</caption>
        <image source="ch-chapter01-equations-09-54-13.png" width="60%">
          <shortdescription>Fluid volume with outward normal and velocity on a boundary patch</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>We want to prove the following result, which essentially equates the change in mass, due to
        density changes, to the flow of mass in or out of the volume.</p>

      <theorem>
        <title>Integral form of the law of conservation of mass</title>

        <statement>
          <p>Given a fixed volume <m>V</m> with boundary <m>\partial V</m>, the integral form
            of the law of mass conservation is <men xml:id="eqn-integralconservation">
              \dd{}{t} \iiint_V \rho \, \de{V} = -\iint_{\partial V} \rho \bu \cdot \bn \, \de{S}.
            </men>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
          <p> The rate of change of mass in <m>V</m> is <me>
              \dd{}{t} \iiint_V \rho(\bx, t) \, \de V = \iiint_V \pd{\rho}{t} \, \de V,
            </me>
            and note the derivative passes through the integral since the volume, <m>V</m>, does not
            change with time. </p>
          <p> Let the boundary of <m>V</m> be given by <m>\partial V</m>, and let <m>\bn</m> denote
            the outward unit normal defined along the boundary <m>\partial V</m>. At each point on
            the boundary, the volume flow rate per unit area (known as the <em>flux</em>) across the
            boundary is given by <m>\bu \cdot \bn</m>, and therefore the mass flow rate per unit
            area is <m>\rho \bu \cdot \bn</m>. </p>
          <p> We now sum the total mass flow across the entire boundary. This is given by the
            surface integral <me>
              \text{rate of mass flow outward across $\partial V$} = \iint_{\partial V} \rho \bu \cdot \bn
            \,
              \de{S}.
            </me>
            The flux out of the boundary is also sketched in <xref ref="fig-normalvolume" />. </p>

          <p> Finally, we apply mass conservation: the rate of change of mass in <m>V</m> must
            equal the rate at which mass flows <em>inward</em> across <m>\partial V</m>. Since the
            surface integral above measures the <em>outward</em> flow, this gives the minus sign in
            <xref ref="eqn-integralconservation" />. </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <p> We now want to transform the integral form in <xref ref="eqn-integralconservation" /> into
        the form of a partial differential equation. To do this, apply the <em>Divergence theorem</em>
        to the right-hand side of the above integral, converting the surface integral to a volume
        integral. Moving all quantities to the left-hand side now yields <me>
          \iiint_V \left[ \pd{\rho}{t} + \nabla \cdot (\rho \bu)\right] \, \de{V} = 0.
        </me>
        Since the above integral equation holds for all possible <m>V</m>, it must be equivalent to
        the integrand equated to zero (<xref ref="lem-bump" />). This recovers the continuity
        equation of <xref ref="thm-mass" />. </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-cor-consvmass">
      <title>Corollary of the Transport Theorem</title>

      <p> There is a useful corollary to Reynolds' Transport Theorem (<xref ref="thm-RTT" />) in
        the case where the integrand is proportional to the density, i.e. <m>f = \rho h</m>. </p>

      <corollary xml:id="cor-convmass">
        <statement>
          <p> Let <m>V(t)</m> be a material volume, let <m>\rho</m> satisfy the continuity equation
            <xref ref="eqn-consvmass1" />, and let <m>h(\bx, t)</m> be any continuously
            differentiable function. Then <men
              xml:id="eqn-cor-convmass">
              \dd{}{t} \iiint_{V(t)} \rho h \, \de{V} = \iiint_{V(t)} \rho \DD{h}{t} \, \de{V}.
            </men>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
          <p> This proof just follows from expansion. Pass the derivative through the integral and
            use the formula of Reynolds' Transport Theorem. Then expand the quantity: <me>
              \nabla \cdot (f\bu) = \nabla f \cdot \bu + f \nabla \cdot \bu,
            </me>
            where <m>f = \rho h</m>. Finally use the continuity equation on <m>\rho</m>. </p>
        </proof>
      </corollary>

    </subsection>
  </section>


  <section xml:id="subsec-euler-momentum">
    <title>Momentum balance</title>

    <introduction>
      <p> With mass conservation now handled, we turn our eyes towards a law that governs the
        conservation of momentum. This is <em>Newton's second law</em>: the rate of change of
        momentum of a body is equal to the sum of all forces acting on the body. </p>


      <p>
        Our task in this section is to prove the following equation for the conservation of
        momentum of a fluid:</p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-momentum">
        <title>Momentum equation</title>
        <statement>
          <p> The differential form of the law of conservation of momentum is <men
              xml:id="eqn-consvmoment">
              \rho \DD{\bu}{t} = \rho \left(\pd{\bu}{t} + (\bu \cdot \nabla)\bu\right) = - \nabla p +
            \rho \bg,
            </men>
            where <m>\rho = \rho(\bx,t)</m> is the density of the fluid, <m>\bu = \bu(\bx, t)</m> is
            the velocity of the fluid, <m>p = p(\bx, t)</m> is the pressure, and <m>\bg</m> is the
            acceleration due to a body force (typically gravity). </p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>

      <p> Note that the term <m>(\bu \cdot \nabla)\bu</m> above can also be written as <m>\bu
        \cdot \nabla\bu</m>, using the notation <m>\nabla \bu</m> for the gradient of a vector.
        This is explained in <xref ref="remark-vector-gradient" />. </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection>
      <title>Proof of the momentum equation for an inviscid fluid</title>


      <p> Consider again a material volume <m>V(t)</m> of the fluid. Then the rate of change of net
        momentum of this volume is equal to <men xml:id="eqn-momentum1">
          \dd{}{t} \iiint_{V(t)} \rho \bu(\bx, t) \, \de{V}.
        </men> It is helpful
        to remember that the mass of a volume element is <m>\rho \de{V}</m> and the acceleration of a fluid
        particle is <m>\DD{\bu}{t}</m>, so the above is similar to mass times acceleration. However, we work with
        the more general form above to allow for changes in the density throughout the fluid, and
        also for variable fluid elements, <m>V(t)</m>. </p>


      <p>
        We must equate the above to the sum of all surface and body forces applied to the fluid
        element.
      </p>

      <p> An example of a body force is the force of gravity. For a small volume element of mass <m>\rho
        \de{V}</m>, the force of gravity is equal to <m>(\rho \de{V})\bg</m>. Therefore, the total
        external force due to gravity on the volume is equal to <men xml:id="eqn-momentum2">
        \iiint_{V(t)} \rho \bg \, \de{V}.
        </men> There may be other external body
        forces. For example, if your fluid is electrically conductive (like a plasma), there may be
        electromagnetic forces that must be considered. In any case, <m>\bg</m> can be considered as
        the analogous body force per unit mass. </p>

      <p> The final type of forces we should consider are <em>surface forces</em>, which are applied
        to the boundary of the fluid element, denoted <m>\partial V</m>. Let us assume that at each
        point on the boundary, there is a surface force per unit area, <m>\bF</m>, that
        decomposes into a component normal to the boundary, <m>F_n \bn</m>, and a component tangential
        to the boundary, <m>F_t \bt</m>. So the surface forces, summed over the boundary, will be <men
          xml:id="eqn-momentum-force">
          \iint_{\partial V} \left[ F_n \bn + F_t \bt\right] \, \de{S}.
        </men> In
        the above, the interpretation is that the force on a small patch of surface area <m>\de{S}</m>
        is equal to the per-area force, say <m>F_n</m>, multiplied by the area, then directed into
        the normal direction, <m>\bn</m>. </p>

      <p> At this point, we make a key assumption that is applied in the particular case of <em>inviscid
        fluids</em>.</p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-inviscid-force">
        <title>Forces in inviscid fluids</title>
        <statement>
          <p> In the case of <em>inviscid fluids</em>, we assume that the surface force exerted
            on (interior) volume elements is accounted for solely by a pressure, <m>p</m>, which acts in
            the inward normal direction at each point, with <me>F_n \bn = -p \bn.
            </me></p>
            
            <p>Consequently,
            the surface force, given by <xref ref="eqn-momentum-force" />, is 
            <men
              xml:id="eqn-momentum-pressure">
              \iint_{\partial V} (-p) \bn \, \de{S} = \iiint_V (-\nabla p) \, \de{V}.
            </men>
          </p>
          <p>
            In particular, for the case of inviscid fluids, we ignore tangential internal forces.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
          <p> The divergence theorem in its usual form applies to the flux of a vector field,
            <m>\iint_{\partial V} \boldsymbol{F} \cdot \bn \, \de{S} = \iiint_V \nabla \cdot
            \boldsymbol{F} \, \de{V}</m>, whereas the left-hand side of <xref
            ref="eqn-momentum-pressure" /> is a vector. We therefore take its dot product with an
            arbitrary constant vector <m>\boldsymbol{c}</m>, which reduces the problem to scalars. </p>

          <p> Since <m>\boldsymbol{c}</m> is constant, it may be moved inside the integral, giving <me>
            \boldsymbol{c} \cdot \iint_{\partial V} (-p) \bn \, \de{S} = \iint_{\partial V} (-p
            \boldsymbol{c}) \cdot \bn \, \de{S}.
            </me> Now apply the divergence theorem with <m>\boldsymbol{F} = -p\boldsymbol{c}</m>.
            Again using that <m>\boldsymbol{c}</m> is constant, <m>\nabla \cdot (-p \boldsymbol{c}) =
            -\boldsymbol{c} \cdot \nabla p</m>, and hence <me>
            \iint_{\partial V} (-p \boldsymbol{c}) \cdot \bn \, \de{S} = \iiint_V (-\boldsymbol{c}
            \cdot \nabla p) \, \de{V} = \boldsymbol{c} \cdot \iiint_V (-\nabla p) \, \de{V}.
            </me> Combining the two results, <me>
            \boldsymbol{c} \cdot \left[ \iint_{\partial V} (-p) \bn \, \de{S} - \iiint_V (-\nabla p)
            \, \de{V} \right] = 0.
            </me></p>

          <p> This holds for every constant vector <m>\boldsymbol{c}</m>. Choosing <m>\boldsymbol{c}
            = \be_1, \be_2, \be_3</m> in turn shows that each component of the bracketed vector is
            zero, so the vector itself is zero, which is <xref ref="eqn-momentum-pressure" />. </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <p> Finally, summing the forces above, Newton's second law gives <me>
        \dd{}{t} \iiint_{V(t)} \rho \bu(\bx, t) \, \de{V} = -\iiint_{V(t)} \nabla p \, \de{V} +
        \iiint_{V(t)} \rho \bg \, \de{V}.
        </me>
        We can now apply <xref ref="cor-convmass" /> to each component of momentum, taking <m>h =
        u, v, w</m> in turn (i.e. <m>f = \rho u, \rho v, \rho w</m>). This allows us to conclude
        that <me>
          \iiint_{V(t)} \left( \rho \DD{\bu}{t} + \nabla p - \rho \bg\right) \, \de{V} = 0.
        </me>
        Again, the above holds for all material volumes <m>V(t)</m> and therefore it must follow that
        the integrand is zero. We conclude thus with the following result as stated in <xref
          ref="thm-momentum" />: <me>
          \rho \DD{\bu}{t} = - \nabla p + \rho \bg.
        </me>
      </p>

      <p> In <xref ref="ex-hw-euler-check" />, you will practise the derivation of the mass and
        momentum equations. </p>
    </subsection>

  </section>
  <section xml:id="sec-incompressible">
    <title>The Euler equations</title>

    <introduction>
      <p> The conservation laws derived so far, for mass (<xref ref="thm-mass" />) and momentum
        (<xref ref="thm-momentum" />), hold for any inviscid fluid, but they do not yet form a
        closed system: there are more unknowns than equations. In this section, we close the system
        by introducing the notion of an <em>incompressible</em> fluid and, in particular, assuming
        that the density is constant. The result is the <em>Euler equations</em>, first written down
        by Leonhard Euler in 1757. Together with the boundary conditions at the end of this section,
        they form a complete mathematical model of an ideal liquid. </p>

      <p> The Euler equations are the central model for much of this course. In <xref
          ref="sec-bernoulli" />, we integrate them to obtain Bernoulli's equation, which links
        pressure to velocity. When the flow is also irrotational, they reduce to Laplace's equation
        for a velocity potential, which is the basis of <xref ref="ch-chapter04-potentialflows" />
        and the water wave theory of <xref ref="ch-chapter05-waves" />. Finally, in <xref
          ref="ch-viscousflows" />, the Euler equations are extended to the Navier–Stokes
        equations by adding the effects of viscosity. </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection>
      <title>Incompressible fluids and the Euler equations</title>


      <p> Recall from <xref ref="thm-jacobian" /> and the proof of Reynolds' Transport Theorem that
        the Jacobian relates infinitesimal volumes in the Eulerian and Lagrangian frames via <men
          xml:id="eq-dxyz-Jacobian">
          \dxyz = J \dXYZ.
        </men> So the Jacobian is a measure of the local
        expansion or contraction of a fluid (relative to its original state). Combining this with
        Euler's identity (<xref ref="thm-Euleridentity" />), we are led to an important interpretation for what it means for a
        fluid to satisfy <m>\nabla \cdot \bu = 0</m>. This leads us to defining the notion of an <em>incompressible
        fluid.</em> (You have already encountered an intuitive definition for an
        incompressible flow as part of <xref ref="ex-video-flow-visualisation" />). </p>

      <theorem xml:id="def-incompressible">
        <title>Incompressible fluids</title>
        <statement>
          <p>A fluid is said to be <em>incompressible</em> if it preserves infinitesimal volumes.
            Since such volumes are related via <xref ref="eq-dxyz-Jacobian" />, then this is
            equivalent to <me>
              \DD{J}{t} \equiv 0,
            </me> within the relevant fluid (and
            temporal) domain.</p>

          <p>The following equivalence (iff) then follows: <men xml:id="eqn-incompressibledef">
            \textrm{Fluid is incompressible} \quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad \nabla \cdot \bu = 0.
            </men>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
          <p> Note that if <m>\DD{J}{t} = 0</m>, then indeed an infinitesimal volume element
            must have constant volume for all time according to <xref ref="eq-dxyz-Jacobian" />. Then
            by Euler's identity in <xref ref="thm-Euleridentity" />, this implies <m>\nabla \cdot
              \bu = 0.</m> Conversely, if <m>\nabla \cdot \bu = 0</m>, then again by the identity
            the fluid is incompressible. </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>


      <p> So in conclusion, for an incompressible fluid, the mass conservation equation of <xref
          ref="thm-mass" /> reduces to <men xml:id="eqn-divu">
          \nabla \cdot \bu = 0,
        </men> together with <m>\DD{\rho}{t} = 0</m>, as shown in the corollary below. If, in
        addition, the density is constant, then it suffices to solve <xref ref="eqn-divu" />. </p>

      <p> To see this, consider what happens to the mass conservation equation in <xref
          ref="thm-mass" /> if the fluid is incompressible. This leads to the following
        corollary.</p>

      <corollary xml:id="cor-incompressible-density">
        <title>Constant density following the flow</title>
        <statement>
          <p> For an incompressible fluid, the density of each fluid particle is constant in time
            (i.e. constant along particle paths): <me>
            \DD{\rho}{t} = \pd{\rho}{t} + (\bu \cdot \nabla)\rho = 0.
            </me>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
          <p> This follows from setting <m>\nabla \cdot \bu = 0</m> in <xref ref="eqn-consvmass2" />. </p>
        </proof>
      </corollary>

      <p> Let us now count equations and unknowns. We have the scalar mass conservation equation of
        <xref ref="thm-mass" /> (or alternatively the simpler equation <xref ref="eqn-divu" /> for
        incompressible fluids), together with the vector momentum equation of <xref
          ref="thm-momentum" />. This yields four scalar equations
        for five unknowns: the pressure <m>p</m>, density <m>\rho</m>, and the three velocity
        components <m>\bu</m>. </p>

      <p> One way of proceeding is to attempt to establish or to intuit a relationship between
        pressure and density. For instance, for a gas, one may use the ideal gas law, <m>p =
        RT\rho</m> (which can be derived from kinetic theory and is well supported empirically),
        relating pressure linearly to density, and depending on the temperature, <m>T</m>, and a (gas)
        constant <m>R</m>. Other assumptions of the form <m>p = p(\rho)</m> are possible, and this
        is involved in the study of gases and <em>compressible fluids</em>. </p>


      <p>
        However, empirically, we observe that in most liquids, the density only varies within a few
        percent under typical variations of temperature and pressure. Therefore, it is common to
        assume:</p>

      <note>
        <title>Constant density assumption</title>
        <p> For liquids, we often assume that the density is constant, <m>\rho =
          \textrm{constant}</m>. </p>
      </note>

      <p> Note, then, that in the case of constant-density fluids, if we consider the mass
        conservation equation <xref ref="eqn-consvmass1" />, it follows that <m>\nabla \cdot \bu = 0</m>.
        Therefore, from the definition <xref ref="def-incompressible" />, we conclude that the fluid
        is indeed incompressible. </p>

      <remark>
        <p> Confusingly, many references define a fluid to be incompressible if <m>\rho</m> is
          constant. However, incompressibility does not require constant density: a fluid can be
          incompressible, so that <m>\DD{\rho}{t} = 0</m>, without <m>\rho</m> being constant. An
          example is the ocean, where density varies with depth due to salinity and
          temperature. </p>
      </remark>

      <p> We are finally ready to state the <em>Euler equations</em>.</p>

      <definition xml:id="def-euler">
        <title>Euler equations</title>

        <statement>
          <p> The <em>Euler equations</em> consist of the incompressibility condition <xref
              ref="eqn-divu" /> and the momentum equations <xref ref="eqn-consvmoment" />,
            considered in the situation of an incompressible fluid of constant density: <mdn>
              <mrow>\nabla \cdot \bu &amp;= 0, </mrow>
              <mrow xml:id="eqn-euler-momentum">
                \left(\pd{\bu}{t} + (\bu \cdot \nabla)\bu\right) &amp;= - \frac{1}{\rho}\nabla p +
                \bg.
              </mrow>
            </mdn>
            These are four scalar equations for four unknowns: the three components of <m>\bu</m>
            and the pressure <m>p</m>. Although incompressibility does not in general require
            constant density (see the remark above), in this course we shall take incompressible
            fluids to have constant density, <m>\rho</m>. The above Euler equations include the (gravitational)
            body force <m>\bg</m>. </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p> Simple applications of the Euler equations are given in <xref
          ref="ex-hw-fluid-pressure-uniform-velocity" /> and <xref ref="ex-hw-piston-fluid-problem" />.
      </p>

      <p> You will consider the derivation of the Euler equations as part of <xref
          ref="ex-hw-euler-check" />. </p>

      <example>
        <title>Computer graphics and fluid simulations</title>
        <p> There is a long-standing link between the research field of fluid mechanics and
          the entertainment field, where cutting-edge applications in animation, movie, and
          video-game graphics use ideas from fluid mechanics to model fluids. Many fluid simulators
          will use things like the Euler equations (or simplified versions of them) to simulate fluids. In
          some cases, such simulations can even happen in real time. Here is an example of a
          real-time Euler equation solver <url
            href="https://matthias-research.github.io/pages/tenMinutePhysics/17-fluidSim.html">coded
          in JavaScript.</url>
        </p>

      </example>
    </subsection>


    <subsection xml:id="subsec-euler-BCs">
      <title>Boundary conditions</title>
      <idx>
        <h>boundary conditions</h>
      </idx>

      <p> A great deal of the complexity of fluid motion comes from the conditions that we must
        consider between the fluid and its bounding surfaces. For water in the ocean, bounding
        surfaces might range from the ocean floor and the hull of a boat, down to the vegetation
        in the water or the sand on a beach. <em>This can get quite complex!</em> In this module,
        and when we first learn fluid dynamics, we only consider simple bounded fluid regions (fluid
        above a plate, fluid in a box, fluid in a channel, etc.).</p>

      <p>In the case of a water wave, a bounding surface will also include the free surface of the
        wave, itself, which interacts with the surrounding atmospheric gas. This leads to the
        situation of free-boundary or free-surface conditions<idx>
          <h>free-surface</h>
        </idx>. </p>

      <p>
        At a solid boundary, we impose the following condition. </p>

      <note xml:id="note-noflux-BC">
        <title>No-flux condition through a boundary</title>
        <p> For a fluid in contact with a fixed rigid boundary, <m>\partial V</m>, we require
          that the normal velocity of the fluid there must be zero: <men
            xml:id="eqn-motion-noflux-BC">
            <idx>
              <h>boundary conditions, no penetration</h>
            </idx> \bu \cdot \bn = 0 \quad
          \textrm{on $\partial V$}, </men> where <m>\bn</m> is the unit normal to <m>\partial V</m>.
          This condition states that the fluid cannot flow through <m>\partial V</m> or separate
          from <m>\partial V</m> (hence leaving a vacuum). </p>
      </note>

      <p> It turns out that the above no-flux condition is sufficient to provide well-posed boundary
        conditions for most inviscid fluids. Note that in particular, we have not constrained the
        tangential velocity of the fluid, i.e. <m>\bu \cdot \bt</m>, where <m>\bt</m> is any
        tangent vector to the boundary. </p>

      <p> Later in <xref ref="ch-viscousflows" />, you will study the situation of a <em>viscous
        fluid</em>, where it will be important to consider the tangential fluid velocity. </p>

    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-bernoulli">
    <title>Bernoulli's equation</title>

    <introduction>
      <p> The Euler equations are a system of nonlinear partial differential equations, in which
        the velocity <m>\bu</m> and the pressure <m>p</m> are coupled together. Solving them in
        general is difficult. In many problems of interest, however, the velocity can be found by
        other means. For example: <ul>
          <li>
            <p> in flow through a pipe or out of a tank, mass conservation often determines the
              speed; </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p> in <xref ref="ch-chapter04-potentialflows" />, the velocity follows from solving
              Laplace's equation; </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p> in <xref ref="ch-chapter05-waves" />, the motion of the free surface is linked to
              the velocity by a kinematic condition. </p>
          </li>
        </ul> In each case, the remaining task is to find the <em>pressure</em>, for instance to
        calculate the force on a body, or to impose that the pressure is atmospheric at a free
        surface. </p>

      <p> Bernoulli's equation does exactly this. It is a <em>first integral</em> of the momentum
        equation: rather than a differential equation, it is an algebraic relation between the
        pressure, the speed and the height of the fluid, <me>
          B = \frac{p}{\rho} + \frac{1}{2}|\bu|^2 + \chi.
        </me> In this section, we first rewrite the momentum equation in a form from which <m>B</m>
        emerges naturally. We then show that, under different assumptions, <m>B</m> is constant
        along streamlines, constant everywhere, or a function of time only. Finally, we apply these
        results to some practical problems. </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-bernoulli-first-integral">
      <title>A first integral of the momentum equation</title>

      <p>
        We will first need a vector identity.</p>

      <identity xml:id="ident-ugradu">
        <statement>
          <p> Note the following identity: <men xml:id="eqn-ugradu">
              (\bu \cdot \nabla) \bu = \nabla \left(\frac{1}{2}|\bu|^2\right) + (\nabla \times \bu)
            \times \bu.
            </men>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            The proof follows from direct expansion of both sides.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </identity>

      <p> The second term on the right-hand side involves the curl of the velocity, which has its
        own name. </p>

      <definition xml:id="def-vorticity">
        <title>Vorticity</title>
        <statement>
          <p> The <em>vorticity</em>, <m>\bomega</m>, of the velocity field <m>\bu</m> is defined by <men
              xml:id="eqn-vorticity">
              \bomega \equiv \nabla \times \bu.
            </men> The vorticity is a
            measure of the local rotation of the flow. </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <definition xml:id="def-irrotational">
        <title>Irrotational flows</title>
        <statement>
          <p> A flow is said to be <em>irrotational</em> if the vorticity is identically zero: <men
              xml:id="eqn-irrotational">
              \textrm{flow is irrotational} \Longleftrightarrow \bomega = \nabla \times \bu \equiv
              \mathbf{0}.
            </men>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p> In <xref ref="ch-chapter04-potentialflows" />, we will show that an irrotational flow in a
        simply connected region can always be written as <m>\bu = \nabla\phi</m> for some scalar
        function <m>\phi</m>, called the <em>velocity potential</em> (<xref
          ref="thm-exist-potential" />). We use this fact below. </p>

      <p> Let us recall the definition of a <em>conservative force</em>.</p>

      <definition xml:id="def-conservative-force">
        <title>Conservative forces</title>
        <statement>
          <p> A force <m>\bF</m> is a <em>conservative force</em> if and only if there exists a
            potential <m>\chi</m>, such that <me>
              \bF = -\nabla \chi,
            </me> in a simply connected region where
            the quantities are defined. </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p> Note the restriction to a simply connected region. The above is not quite the usual
        definition of a conservative force (typically defined to be a force for which the work done
        on an object between two points is independent of path). For now, this is not an important
        distinction since we will focus on fluid regions that are free of holes. Until told
        otherwise, we will always assume that the fluid is simply connected and therefore the above
        serves as a definition of conservative forces.
      </p>

      <remark>
        <p> For the typical gravity force directed in the <m>-z</m> direction, we can write <m>\bg =
          -g[0, 0, 1] = -g \be_z</m>. So in this case, the (gravitational) potential is <m>\chi = gz</m>. </p>
      </remark>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-bernoulli-form">
        <title>The momentum equation in Bernoulli form</title>
        <statement>
          <p> Consider an inviscid, incompressible fluid of constant density <m>\rho</m>, subject
            to a conservative body force <m>\bg = -\nabla\chi</m>. Define the <em>Bernoulli
            function</em> <men xml:id="eqn-bernoulli-B">
              B(\bx, t) \equiv \frac{p}{\rho} + \frac{1}{2}|\bu|^2 + \chi.
            </men> Then the momentum equation <xref ref="eqn-euler-momentum" /> can be written as <men
              xml:id="eqn-bernoulli-vorticity">
              \pd{\bu}{t} + \bomega \times \bu = -\nabla B.
            </men>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
          <p> Start from the momentum equation <xref ref="eqn-euler-momentum" />, with <m>\bg =
            -\nabla \chi</m>, <me>
              \pd{\bu}{t} + (\bu \cdot \nabla)\bu = - \frac{1}{\rho}\nabla p - \nabla \chi.
            </me>
            Using the vector identity <xref ref="eqn-ugradu" />, and noting that <m>\frac{1}{\rho}\nabla
            p = \nabla(p/\rho)</m> since <m>\rho</m> is constant, gives
            <men xml:id="eqn-bernoulli-unsteady-chap3">
              \pd{\bu}{t} + (\nabla \times \bu) \times \bu = - \nabla \left(\frac{p}{\rho} +
            \frac{1}{2} |\bu|^2 + \chi\right).
            </men>
            Using the definitions of the vorticity <xref ref="eqn-vorticity" /> and of <m>B</m> <xref
              ref="eqn-bernoulli-B" /> gives the result. </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <p> Every version of Bernoulli's equation follows from <xref ref="eqn-bernoulli-vorticity" />
        by removing terms from the left-hand side. </p>

      <aside>
        <title>Removed in 2026</title>
        <p>In previous years, this section also derived the <em>vorticity equation</em>, <m>\DD{\bomega}{t}
          = (\bomega \cdot \nabla)\bu</m>, which describes how vorticity evolves in an inviscid
          fluid. This was removed from the notes in 2026 and is no longer part of the course.</p>
      </aside>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-bernoulli-forms">
      <title>Three forms of Bernoulli's equation</title>

      <p> <xref ref="table-bernoulli-forms" /> summarises the three forms of Bernoulli's equation
        derived in this subsection, together with where each is used in the course. In each case,
        the assumptions of <xref ref="thm-bernoulli-form" /> also apply. </p>

      <table xml:id="table-bernoulli-forms">
        <title>The three forms of Bernoulli's equation</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="major">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Additional assumptions</cell>
            <cell>Conclusion</cell>
            <cell>Used in</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Steady</cell>
            <cell><m>B</m> is constant along each streamline</cell>
            <cell><xref ref="subsec-bernoulli-applications" text="global" /></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Steady and irrotational</cell>
            <cell><m>B</m> is constant everywhere</cell>
            <cell><xref ref="ch-chapter04-potentialflows" text="global" /></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Irrotational, possibly unsteady</cell>
            <cell><m>\pd{\phi}{t} + B = F(t)</m> everywhere</cell>
            <cell><xref ref="ch-chapter05-waves" text="global" /></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-bernoulli-steady">
        <title>Bernoulli's equation for steady flow</title>
        <statement>
          <p> For a steady flow, <men xml:id="eqn-bernoulli-steady">
              B(\bx) = \frac{p}{\rho} + \frac{1}{2} |\bu|^2 + \chi = \textrm{constant along
            streamlines}.
            </men>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
          <p> Since the flow is steady, the first term of <xref ref="eqn-bernoulli-vorticity" />
            vanishes. This leaves <me>
            \bomega \times \bu = -\nabla B.
            </me> We now take the dot
            product of both sides of the equation with <m>\bu</m>. We use the fact that, <me>
              \bu \cdot (\bomega \times \bu ) = 0,
            </me> since it is a
            triple scalar product with two repeated entries. It therefore results in the fact that <me>
            \bu \cdot \nabla B = 0.
            </me> Since <m>B</m> does not depend on time, <m>\DD{B}{t} = \bu \cdot \nabla B</m>, so this
            states that <m>\DD{B}{t} = 0</m>. Hence <m>B</m> is constant along streamlines of the
            flow (which, for steady flow, coincide with particle paths). </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-bernoulli-potential">
        <title>Bernoulli's equation for steady irrotational flow</title>
        <statement>
          <p> For a steady and irrotational flow, <men xml:id="eqn-bernoulli-potential">
              B(\bx) = \frac{p}{\rho} + \frac{1}{2} |\bu|^2 + \chi = \textrm{constant
            everywhere}.
            </men>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
          <p> For steady flow, the first term of <xref ref="eqn-bernoulli-vorticity" /> vanishes.
            If, in addition, the flow is irrotational, then <m>\bomega = \mathbf{0}</m>, and
            therefore <me>
            \nabla B = \mathbf{0}.
            </me> Since all spatial derivatives of <m>B</m> are zero, <m>B</m> is constant
            throughout the (connected) fluid domain. </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-bernoulli-unsteady-potential">
        <title>Bernoulli's equation for unsteady potential flow</title>
        <statement>
          <p> For an irrotational, possibly unsteady, flow with velocity potential <m>\phi</m>, so
            that <m>\bu = \nabla\phi</m>, <men xml:id="eqn-bernoulli-unsteady-potential">
              \pd{\phi}{t} + \frac{1}{2}|\nabla\phi|^2 + \frac{p}{\rho} + \chi = F(t),
            </men> for some function <m>F(t)</m>. </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
          <p> Set <m>\bomega = \mathbf{0}</m> and <m>\bu = \nabla\phi</m> in <xref
              ref="eqn-bernoulli-vorticity" />. Since space and time derivatives commute, <m>\pd{}{t}(\nabla
            \phi) = \nabla\left(\pd{\phi}{t}\right)</m>, and so <me>
              \nabla\left( \pd{\phi}{t} + \frac{1}{2}|\nabla\phi|^2 + \frac{p}{\rho} + \chi
              \right) = \mathbf{0}.
            </me> The quantity in brackets therefore has no spatial dependence, i.e. it is a
            function of <m>t</m> alone. </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <remark>
        <title>The function <m>F(t)</m></title>
        <p> The function <m>F(t)</m> can be absorbed into the potential. Replacing <m>\phi</m> by <m>\phi
          - \int F \, \de{t}</m> does not change the velocity <m>\bu = \nabla\phi</m>, but sets <m>F
          \equiv 0</m>. Equivalently, <m>F(t)</m> may be chosen for convenience, as is done in
          <xref ref="ch-chapter05-waves" />. </p>
      </remark>

      <remark>
        <title>Constant <m>B</m> on every streamline</title>
        <p> In the steady case, <m>B</m> can be constant everywhere even when the flow is not
          irrotational. This happens in many practical problems, where every streamline starts in
          a large reservoir in which the fluid is at rest, so that <m>B</m> takes the same value on
          every streamline. </p>
      </remark>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-bernoulli-applications">
      <title>Applications</title>

      <p> In each example below, we state which form of Bernoulli's equation is used and why its
        assumptions hold. </p>

      <example xml:id="ex-bernoulli-hydrostatics">
        <title>Hydrostatics and Pascal's bursting barrel</title>

        <p> For a fluid at rest, <m>\bu = \mathbf{0}</m>. The flow is then trivially steady and
          irrotational, so <xref ref="thm-bernoulli-potential" /> gives <me>
            \frac{p}{\rho} + gz = \textrm{constant everywhere}.
          </me> If the free surface is at <m>z = H</m>, where <m>p = p_\text{atm}</m>, then <me>
            p = p_\text{atm} + \rho g (H - z).
          </me> The pressure at depth <m>d = H - z</m> is <m>p_\text{atm} + \rho g d</m>. It
          depends only on depth, not on the shape of the container or the amount of fluid above. </p>

        <p> This explains <em>Pascal's bursting barrel</em>. A long thin vertical tube is attached
          to a sealed barrel full of water, and the tube is then filled. Although the column in the
          tube holds very little water, it raises the pressure throughout the barrel by <m>\rho g
          h</m>. For a <m>10</m> m column, that is about <m>10^5</m> Pa, roughly one atmosphere.
          Acting on a lid of diameter <m>0.5</m> m, this is a force of about <m>2 \times 10^4</m> N,
          the weight of roughly two tonnes. </p>

        <p>Watch <url
            href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJHrr21UvY8">this short video demonstration from
          Princeton</url>, showing the power of hydrostatic pressure.</p>
      </example>

      <example xml:id="ex-bernoulli-torricelli">
        <title>Torricelli's law</title>

        <statement>
          <p> A large open tank of cross-sectional area <m>A</m> holds water of depth <m>h(t)</m>
            above a small hole of area <m>a \ll A</m> in its base. </p>
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
              <p> Find the speed <m>u</m> of the jet leaving the hole. </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p> If the initial depth is <m>h_0</m>, find the time for the tank to drain. </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            <alert>(a)</alert> The free surface falls at speed <m>U</m>, where mass conservation
            gives <m>AU = au</m>, so <m>U/u = a/A \ll 1</m>. The flow therefore changes slowly, and
            we treat it as steady. Taking a streamline from the free surface (<m>z = h</m>) to the
            jet (<m>z = 0</m>), where the pressure is atmospheric at both ends, <xref
              ref="thm-bernoulli-steady" /> gives <me>
              \frac{p_\text{atm}}{\rho} + \frac{1}{2}U^2 + gh = \frac{p_\text{atm}}{\rho} +
              \frac{1}{2}u^2.
            </me> Using <m>U = (a/A)u</m>, <me>
              u = \sqrt{\frac{2gh}{1 - (a/A)^2}} \approx \sqrt{2gh}.
            </me> This is <em>Torricelli's law</em> (1643): the jet leaves at the speed of a
            particle falling freely from height <m>h</m>. </p>

          <p>
            <alert>(b)</alert> Mass conservation gives <me>
              A \dd{h}{t} = -a\sqrt{2gh}.
            </me> Separating variables and using <m>h(0) = h_0</m> gives <me>
              \sqrt{h} = \sqrt{h_0} - \frac{a}{A}\sqrt{\frac{g}{2}} \, t,
            </me> so the tank empties at time <me>
              T = \frac{A}{a}\sqrt{\frac{2h_0}{g}}.
            </me>
          </p>

          <p> In practice, the jet from a sharp-edged hole contracts just after leaving it (the
            <em>vena contracta</em>), and the measured flow rate is about <m>0.6</m> of this
            prediction. </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <p> You will practise an application of Bernoulli's equation in <xref
          ref="ex-bernoulli-streamline" />, which combines the calculation of <xref
          ref="ex-bernoulli-torricelli" /> with a choice of jar shape that makes the water level
        fall at a constant rate. </p>

      <example>
        <title>Urination across species</title>

        <p>Watch <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EidLGwyYpBE">this short
          video describing</url> the work by Yang et al. on <url
            href="https://www.pnas.org/doi/pdf/10.1073/pnas.1402289111">"Duration of urination does
          not change with body size"</url>. </p>

        <p> Yang et al. model the bladder as a pressurised reservoir that drains through a long,
          narrow tube, the urethra. Applying Bernoulli's equation between the bladder and the exit
          of the urethra, gravity acting over the length of the urethra adds to the bladder
          pressure, much as the depth <m>h</m> drives the jet in Torricelli's law (<xref
            ref="ex-bernoulli-torricelli" />). Larger animals have longer urethras, so urine leaves
          faster, which compensates for their larger bladders. As a result, the duration of
          urination is roughly the same, about <m>21</m> s, for animals heavier than about <m>3</m>
          kg. For smaller animals, viscosity and surface tension dominate and urine is released as
          drops, so this argument no longer applies. </p>
      </example>

      <example>
        <title>The limits of Bernoulli's equation: airflow into or out of a room</title>

        <p> Watch <url
            href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1L2ef1CP-yw">this short video by Matthias Wandel</url>
          about airflow into and out of a room. </p>

        <p> Bernoulli's equation treats flow into and out of an opening in the same way, but in
          practice the two look very different. Air pushed out through an opening forms a narrow
          jet that travels a long way, whereas air drawn in through an opening arrives from all
          directions. The difference comes from the flow <em>separating</em> at the edges of the
          opening, a viscous effect that inviscid theory cannot capture. We meet viscosity in <xref
            ref="ch-viscousflows" />. </p>
      </example>

    </subsection>
  </section>

  <exercises xml:id="ws-equations">

    <introduction>

      <remark xml:id="remark-solutions-feedback-equations">
        <title>Solutions and feedback</title>
        <p>Full solutions to these exercises can be found in <xref ref="solutions-equations">the solutions to this chapter</xref> in <xref ref="solutions"/> at the back
          of the book.</p>

        <p>If you would like feedback on your work, the tutor will by default concentrate on the
          exercises highlighted with a yellow background in the online notes (the homework
          exercises), unless you specifically ask for feedback on a different exercise instead.</p>
      </remark>

      <p>

        This chapter focused on deriving the conservation laws for a fluid from first principles
        using vector calculus, leading to the Euler equations for an inviscid and incompressible
        flow. We then studied different forms of the Euler equations and their consequences.
      </p>

      <aside>
        <title>Removed in 2026</title>
        <p>A problem on deriving the vorticity equation was removed from this set of exercises in
          2026, as the vorticity equation is no longer part of the course.</p>
      </aside>

      <!-- <warning>
  <p>We look to complete worksheets in the week prior to the content being delivered. Once this is
      done, this disclaimer message will be removed.</p>
</warning> -->
    </introduction>

