    <exercise xml:id="ex-bump">
      <title>Bump lemma</title>

      <statement>
        <p> Within our derivations of the governing equations, we often make use of the so-called
          "Bump lemma" given in <xref ref="lem-bump" />. </p>
        <p>
          Give a proof of the lemma.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p> This proof assumes that the function <m>f</m> is not pathological (e.g. it cannot
          oscillate infinitely fast, take different values for rational vs. irrational, etc.).
          That's what we mean by "sufficiently smooth". </p>
        <p> Assume that there exists a point <m>\bx_0 \in \Omega</m> where <m>f(\bx_0) \neq 0</m>.
          Suppose without loss of generality <m>f > 0</m> here. Since <m>f</m> is sufficiently
          smooth (and in particular, it is continuous), then there exists a non-trivial
          neighbourhood of <m>\bx_0</m>, say <m>V_0</m>, where <m>f > 0</m> everywhere. Then <m>\int_{V_0}
          f \, \de{V} > 0</m>, contradicting the assumption. Therefore <m>
          f</m> must be zero everywhere. </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-hw-leibnitz-rule">
      <title>Leibniz's rule</title>

      <introduction>
        <p> Reynolds' Transport Theorem <xref ref="thm-RTT" />, which relates to the passage of
          a time derivative through an integral, is a general form of the 1D Leibniz rule: <mdn>
            <mrow number="no">\dd{}{t} &amp; \int_{a(t)}^{b(t)} f(x, t) \, \de{x} </mrow>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-leibnitz-rule"> &amp; = \int_{a(t)}^{b(t)} \pd{f}{t} \, \de{x} +
          f(b(t), t) \dot{b}(t) - f(a(t), t)\dot{a}(t).</mrow>
          </mdn>
        </p>
      </introduction>


      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> Examine the figure in <xref ref="ex-fig-leibnitz" />. Can you associate the quantities
            in Leibniz's rule with the circled elements in the figure?</p>

          <figure xml:id="ex-fig-leibnitz">
            <caption>Visual "proof" of Leibniz's rule</caption>
            <image source="equations_leibnitz.jpg" width="90%">
              <shortdescription>Areas contributing to the change of an integral with moving limits</shortdescription>
            </image>
          </figure>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p> The first circled element is associated with the quantity: <me>
              \frac{1}{\Delta t} \int_{a(t)}^{b(t)} [f(x, t + \Delta t) - f(x, t)] \, \de{x}.
            </me>
            This is essentially the finite-difference version of the first term on the RHS. </p>

          <p> The second circled element is associated with the quantity: <me>
              -f(a(t), t)[a(t + \Delta t) - a(t)] \frac{1}{\Delta t}.
            </me> This is the area of the left-hand (blue) rectangle, which is lost from the
            integral as the lower limit moves to the right, hence the minus sign. </p>

          <p> The third circled element is associated with <me>
              f(b(t), t + \Delta t)[b(t + \Delta t) - b(t)] \frac{1}{\Delta t}.
            </me>
            This is the area of the right-hand (pink) rectangle, which is gained as the upper limit
            moves to the right. Notice that for small <m>\Delta t</m>, the rectangle height <m>f(b,
            t+\Delta t)</m> is approximately <m>f(b, t)</m>. </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> Derive Leibniz's rule <xref ref="eqn-leibnitz-rule" />. You may assume that <m>f</m>
            and <m>\pd{f}{t}</m> are continuous, and that <m>a(t)</m> and <m>b(t)</m> are
            differentiable. </p>
        </statement>

        <hint>
          <p> Let <m>I(t) = \int_{a(t)}^{b(t)} f(x, t) \, \de{x}</m> and start from the definition
            of the derivative, <me>
              \dd{I}{t} = \lim_{\Delta t \to 0} \frac{I(t + \Delta t) - I(t)}{\Delta t}.
            </me> Write <m>I(t + \Delta t)</m> as an integral from <m>a(t + \Delta t)</m> to <m>b(t
            + \Delta t)</m>, and split it into three integrals: one over the fixed interval <m>[a(t),
            b(t)]</m>, and one over each of the two small intervals near the end points. These
            correspond to the three circled regions in <xref ref="ex-fig-leibnitz" />. For small <m>\Delta
            t</m>, the two small intervals are narrow, so each of their integrals can be approximated
            by the area of a single rectangle. </p>

          <p> A fully rigorous proof is not required here. It is fine to use approximations such
            as these, provided you explain why they become exact as <m>\Delta t \to 0</m>. </p>
        </hint>

        <solution>
          <p> Let <m>I(t) = \int_{a(t)}^{b(t)} f(x, t) \, \de{x}</m>. Splitting the range of
            integration, <md>
              <mrow>I(t + \Delta t) &amp;= \int_{a(t + \Delta t)}^{b(t + \Delta t)} f(x, t + \Delta
                t) \, \de{x}</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;= \int_{a(t)}^{b(t)} f(x, t + \Delta t) \, \de{x} + \int_{b(t)}^{b(t +
                \Delta t)} f(x, t + \Delta t) \, \de{x}</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;\quad - \int_{a(t)}^{a(t + \Delta t)} f(x, t + \Delta t) \, \de{x}.</mrow>
            </md> Subtracting <m>I(t)</m> and dividing by <m>\Delta t</m> gives <md>
              <mrow>\frac{I(t + \Delta t) - I(t)}{\Delta t} &amp;= \int_{a(t)}^{b(t)} \frac{f(x, t
                + \Delta t) - f(x, t)}{\Delta t} \, \de{x}</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;\quad + \frac{1}{\Delta t}\int_{b(t)}^{b(t + \Delta t)} f(x, t + \Delta t)
                \, \de{x}</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;\quad - \frac{1}{\Delta t}\int_{a(t)}^{a(t + \Delta t)} f(x, t + \Delta t)
                \, \de{x}.</mrow>
            </md> These three terms correspond to the circled regions 1, 3 and 2 of <xref
              ref="ex-fig-leibnitz" /> respectively. We take the limit <m>\Delta t \to 0</m> in
            each. </p>

          <p>
            <em>First term.</em> For small <m>\Delta t</m>, the integrand is the difference
            quotient of <m>f</m> in time, which is approximately <m>\pd{f}{t}</m>. Since <m>\pd{f}{t}</m>
            is continuous, this approximation improves uniformly across the interval as <m>\Delta t
            \to 0</m>, and the first term tends to <me>
              \int_{a(t)}^{b(t)} \pd{f}{t} \, \de{x}.
            </me>
          </p>

          <p>
            <em>Second term.</em> For small <m>\Delta t</m>, the interval from <m>b(t)</m> to <m>b(t
            + \Delta t)</m> is narrow, with width approximately <m>\dot{b}\,\Delta t</m>, and across
            it <m>f(x, t + \Delta t)</m> is close to <m>f(b(t), t)</m>. We may therefore approximate
            the integral by the area of a single rectangle (a one-term Riemann sum): <me>
              \frac{1}{\Delta t}\int_{b(t)}^{b(t + \Delta t)} f(x, t + \Delta t) \, \de{x} \approx
              f(b(t), t) \, \frac{b(t + \Delta t) - b(t)}{\Delta t}.
            </me> Since <m>f</m> is continuous, the error in this approximation is small compared
            with <m>\Delta t</m>, and so disappears in the limit. The term therefore tends to <m>f(b(t),
            t)\,\dot{b}(t)</m>. </p>

          <p>
            <em>Third term.</em> In the same way, this tends to <m>-f(a(t), t)\,\dot{a}(t)</m>.
          </p>

          <p> Adding the three limits gives Leibniz's rule <xref ref="eqn-leibnitz-rule" />. </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> Can you now connect the form of the rule in <xref ref="eqn-leibnitz-rule" /> to the
            Reynolds' Transport Theorem in <xref ref="eqn-RTT" />? </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p> In one dimension, with <m>a(t)</m> and <m>b(t)</m> moving with the fluid (so
            <m>\dot{a} = u(a, t)</m> and <m>\dot{b} = u(b, t)</m>), Reynolds' Transport Theorem
            reads <me>
              \dd{}{t} \int_{a(t)}^{b(t)} f \, \de{x} = \int_{a(t)}^{b(t)} \left[ \pd{f}{t} +
              \pd{}{x}(f u) \right] \de{x}.
            </me> Integrating the second term gives <me>
              f(b, t)u(b, t) - f(a, t)u(a, t) = f(b, t)\dot{b} - f(a, t)\dot{a},
            </me> which are exactly the second and third terms of Leibniz's rule. </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
    </exercise>

    <!--
<exercise xml:id="ex-rtt-mass-tank">
  <title>Filling tank</title>
  <exercise xml:id="tank-filling">
    <p>
      The tank shown in the diagram below has cross-sectional area <m>A</m>
      and height <m>H</m> and has an inlet at the top left and an outlet halfway
      up on the right-hand side.
      <figure xml:id="fig-tank">
        <caption>Diagram of tank used for filling</caption>
        <image source="ch-chapter07-tank-setup.jpg" width="50%">
          <shortdescription>Filling tank</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </figure>
      Starting from empty, the tank is filled with constant volumetric flux <m>Q</m>
      of water through the inlet, and during filling the water level is at
      height <m>h(t)</m>. There are three phases during the filling:
    <ol>
      <li>The first phase when the level in the tank is lower than the outlet pipe,
        and therefore there is inflow and no outflow;</li>
      <li>The second phase when the water level is above that of the outflow but the
        tank is not full, so there is inflow and outflow (but the outflow must be less
        than the inflow so that the tank continues to fill);</li>
      <li>When the tank is full, the inflow equals the outflow.</li>
    </ol>
    During Phase (ii) you may assume that the flow through the outlet is proportional to the depth of
    fluid above the outlet, that is, the volumetric flux through the outlet is <m>k(h - H/2)</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
    In this question you will find the depth <m>h(t)</m> of the water in the tank over time.
  </p>
  <p>
  <ol>        
    <li>
      <p>
        Use Reynolds' Transport Theorem to find the depth of water in the tank during Phase (i).
    <solution>
      <p>
        We define the control volume <m>V(t)</m> to be the volume of water in the tank and apply
        mass conservation to the system of particles. We set <m>f=\rho</m> to be the mass per unit
        volume, so that
        <me>\iiint_Vf \, \de{x} \, \de{y} \, \de{z}</me>
        is the mass of fluid in the tank. Since the mass 
        We use Reynolds' Transport Theorem <xref ref="eqn-RTT"/>. The left-hand side equals zero
        by mass conservation. Hence
        <me>
          0 = \iiint_{V(t)} \left[\pd{f}{t} + \nabla \cdot (f\bu)\right] \, \de{x} \, \de{y} \, \de{z}.
        </me>
        <me>
          0 = \frac{dV_{CV}}{dt} + Q_{CS} = \frac{dV_{CV}}{dt} + Q_{\textrm{outlet}} - Q_{\textrm{inlet}}.
        </me>
        Since the fluid is incompressible we can use volume conservation
        instead of mass conservation.
        During Phase (i), <m>Q_{\text{outlet}} = 0</m> and <m>Q_{\text{inlet}} = Q</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>0 = \frac{dV_{CV}}{dt} - Q</me>
      <p>
        Substituting <m>V_{CV} = Ah</m>, where <m>h(t)</m> is the depth of fluid at time <m>t</m>, we get
      </p>
      <me>h = \frac{Qt}{A}</me>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>At what time does Phase (ii) start?</statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The outlet is reached when <m>h = H/2</m>, which happens at time <m>t = \tfrac{AH}{2Q}</m>, the
    beginning of Phase (ii).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>Find the height <m>h(t)</m> in the tank during Phase (ii).</statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We apply the RTT as before, so
      </p>
      <me>0 = \frac{dV_{CV}}{dt} + Q_{\text{outlet}} - Q_{\text{inlet}}</me>
      <p>
        but now <m>Q_{\text{outlet}} = k(h - H/2)</m>. Hence
      </p>
      <me>
        \frac{dh}{dt} + \frac{k}{A}h = \frac{1}{A}\left(Q + \tfrac{kH}{2}\right)
      </me>
      <p>
        Solving with integrating factors gives
      </p>
      <me>
        h = \frac{Q}{k} + \frac{H}{2} + c e^{-kt/A}
      </me>
      <p>
        where <m>c</m> is a constant of integration. Noting that <m>h = H/2</m> at <m>t = AH/2Q</m> gives
    <m>c = -\tfrac{Q}{k} e^{kH/2Q}</m>, and thus
      </p>
      <me>
        h = \frac{H}{2} + \frac{Q}{k}\left(1 - e^{kH/2Q} e^{-kt/A}\right)
      </me>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>Assuming that <m>k</m> is small enough that the tank actually fills, how long does Phase
    (ii) last?</statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Phase (ii) ends when the tank is full, that is <m>h = H</m>, which implies
      </p>
      <me>
        t = \frac{AH}{2Q} - \frac{A}{k} \ln\left(1 - \frac{kH}{2Q}\right)
      </me>
      <p>
        Thus Phase (ii) lasts for a time <m> -\tfrac{A}{k} \ln(1 - kH/(2Q))</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>What is the largest possible value of <m>k</m> so that the tank fills?</statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We require <m>kH/(2Q) &lt; 1</m>. Thus the largest value of <m>k</m> is <m>2Q/H</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>Hence sketch a graph of the height of the water in the tank over time.</statement>
    <solution>
      <figure>
        <image source="tank-filling.pdf" />
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      Once the tank reaches the end of Phase (ii), the pressure suddenly rises so as to drive the
    required flow rate <m>Q</m> out of the tank during Phase (iii).
      What height of the fluid in the tank would cause this outflow if it were not forced (that is, if
    the tank were much taller)?
      Where does the force come from that causes this pressure rise?
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let the unknown height be <m>H_0</m>. We require <m>Q = k(H_0 - H/2)</m>, meaning that the required
    height is <m>H_0 = Q/k + H/2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once the tank is full, the pressure in it can rise arbitrarily, because there is no longer
    atmospheric air in it. The pressure in the inlet needs to rise to create the extra pressure
    within the tank.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </p>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>
</exercise>

