    <exercise xml:id="ex-hw-fluid-pressure-uniform-velocity">
      <title>Pressure field in uniform flow</title>
      <introduction>
        <p> An inviscid, incompressible fluid of constant density <m>\rho</m>, in a gravitational
          field with acceleration vector <m>\bg = -g \bk</m> (with <m>\bk</m> being the unit
          vector in the positive <m>z</m>-direction) has uniform velocity <me>
            \bu = (a + b t, \; t^3, \; \e^t).
          </me> The pressure at the origin
          is fixed at <m>
            p_0</m>. </p>
      </introduction>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> Using the Euler equations in <xref ref="def-euler" />, find the pressure field in
            terms of <m>x, y, z, t, a, b, g, \rho</m> and <m>p_0</m>. </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p> The fluid has a uniform velocity, which means that all components of <m>\nabla\bu</m>
            vanish. The Euler equation simplifies to <me>
              \rho\pd{\bu}{t}=-\nabla p-\rho g \bk,
            </me> where <me>
            \pd{\bu}{t}=(b, \; 3t^2, \; \e^t).
            </me> Hence, the pressure
            is given by solving the equation, <me>
              \nabla p = -\rho \pd{\bu}{t} - \rho g \bk
              = -\rho (\, b, \; 3t^2, \; \e^t + g \,),
            </me>
            which is a vector equation. Splitting into components, we obtain <me>
              \frac{\partial p}{\partial x} = -\rho b, \quad
              \frac{\partial p}{\partial y} = -3\rho t^2, \quad
              \frac{\partial p}{\partial z} = -\rho (\e^t + g).
            </me>
            Solving sequentially, the first equation gives <me>
              p = -\rho b x + f_1(y,z,t).
            </me> Substituting into the
            second equation, <me>
              \frac{\partial f_1}{\partial y} = -3\rho t^2
              \;\;\Rightarrow\;\;
              f_1 = -3\rho t^2 y + f_2(z,t),
            </me>
        <me>
            p = -\rho b x - 3\rho t^2 y + f_2(z,t).
            </me> The third
            equation then implies <me>
              \frac{\partial f_2}{\partial z} = -\rho(\e^t+g)
              \;\;\Rightarrow\;\;
              f_2 = -\rho(\e^t+g)z + f_3(t),
            </me>
        <me>
            p = -\rho b x - 3\rho t^2 y - \rho(\e^t+g)z + f_3(t).
            </me>
            Using <m>p
              = p_0</m> at the origin gives <m>f_3(t) = p_0</m> for all <m>t</m>. Hence <me>
              p = p_0 - \rho \big(bx + 3t^2 y + (\e^t + g)z \big).
            </me>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            What can you say about the surfaces of constant pressure? Sketch this at a fixed time <m>t</m>,
            and hence describe the pressure field.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            The pressure is constant on parallel planes normal to the vector
            <m>(b, \; 3t^2, \; \e^t + g)</m>,
            as illustrated below:
          </p>
          <figure xml:id="fig-pressure-contours">
            <caption>Contours of the pressure field at fixed <m>t</m>.</caption>
            <image source="ch-chapter03-pressure-contours.png" />
          </figure>
        </solution>
      </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-hw-piston-fluid-problem">
      <title>Piston problem</title>
      <statement>
        <p> The piston shown in the diagram below is pushed with a force <m>F(t)</m> into a pipe of
          length <m>L</m> and cross-sectional area <m>A</m> containing incompressible and
          irrotational fluid of density <m>\rho</m>. </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-piston">
          <caption>Schematic of piston and pipe system.</caption>
          <image source="ch-chapter03-piston.png" />
        </figure>

        <p>Assume that the pressure at the open end is held at atmospheric pressure, <m>p =
          p_\text{atm}</m>.</p>

        <p> Assume the fluid moves as a rigid body and neglect friction and the mass of the piston.</p>

        <p>Assume that the velocity takes the form of <m>\bu = (X(t), 0, 0)</m> at the position of the piston.</p>

        <p>
          Using the Euler equations in <xref ref="def-euler" />, write down a differential equation
          governing the position, <m>X(t)</m>, of the piston at time <m>t</m>. You may neglect
          gravity. You do not need to solve the equation
          (it could be solved using Python or similar).</p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          We assume that the fluid pressure at the open end of the pipe is atmospheric pressure.
          Since the fluid moves as a rigid body, the velocity field has no spatial gradients, and
          hence the Euler equation reduces to
          <me>
            \rho\pd{\bu}{t}=-\nabla p,
          </me> where the velocity is uniformly <m>\bu=(\dot{X}(t),0)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Solving for <m>p</m> and using the fact that <m>p</m> is atmospheric pressure
          at <m>x = L</m>, we have
          <me>
            p(x,t) = \rho\dd{^2 X}{t^2}(L-x)+p_\text{atm}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          At the piston <m>x=X</m>, note that atmospheric pressure applies in addition to
          <m>F</m>, meaning that the force on the fluid is
          <me>
            F+Ap_\text{atm},
          </me>
          and hence the fluid pressure is
          <me>
            \frac{F}{A} + p_\text{atm}.
          </me>
          Then
          <me>
            \frac{F}{A} + p_\text{atm} = \rho\dd{^2X}{t^2}(L-X)+p_\text{atm}
            \quad\Rightarrow\quad
            \dd{^2X}{t^2} = \frac{F}{A\rho(L-X)}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Note that the atmospheric pressure cancels in this problem,
          and this is common to problems of this type, because atmospheric pressure
          acts uniformly on all surfaces exposed to the atmosphere and therefore does
          not lead to a resultant force or affect the dynamics.
          This motivates the introduction of a gauge pressure, <m>p_{\text{gauge}}</m>,
          whereby a uniform and constant baseline pressure is chosen,
          in this case <m>p_{\text{atm}}</m>, with the gauge pressure given by
          <m>p_{\text{gauge}}=p-p_{\text{atm}}</m>.
          Since the pressure only appears as a gradient in the governing equations,
          using the gauge pressure does not affect the result.
          Introducing a gauge pressure can make the algebra simpler.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-bernoulli-streamline">
      <title>Using streamlines: The clepsydra</title>
      <introduction>
        <p> One of the earliest means for measuring the passage of time, invented by the ancient
          Egyptians, was the <term>clepsydra</term> (or ‘water thief’): a large jar with a hole in
          its base was filled with water. The shape of the jar was such that the interval of time
          taken for the water surface to pass two equally-spaced markers on the side of the jar is
          constant. </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-clepsydra">
          <caption>Clepsydra</caption>
          <image source="clepsydra.png" width="50%">
            <shortdescription>Axisymmetric water clock draining through a hole in its base</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p> In this question you will determine the shape of jar required to achieve this. In
          particular, we denote the radius of the (axisymmetric) jar at height <m>z</m> above the hole by
          <m>r = a(z)</m>. Note that, in general, the radius of the hole, <m>a_h</m>, differs from
          <m>a(0)</m>. </p>
      </introduction>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>Explain why the curve <m>r = a(z)</m> is a streamline.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The fluid velocity at the surface of the jar satisfies <m>\bu\cdot\bn=0</m>,
            so that
            no fluid leaves or enters the jar through its sides. This means that the flow
            next to the boundary of the jar must be parallel to this boundary, meaning
            that streamlines are parallel to the boundary. Equivalently,
            the boundary <m>r = a(z)</m> is a streamline.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> If the surface of the water lies at <m>z = h(t)</m>, use an appropriate form of
            Bernoulli's principle to calculate the speed of liquid, <m>u</m>, leaving the jar at <m>z
            = 0</m>. </p>
          <p> [You may assume that the desired surface speed <m>u_S = |\dot{h}| \ll u</m>, so that
            the flow is approximately steady and that the fluid pressure is atmospheric at <m>z = 0</m>.] </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p> We apply Bernoulli's equation to equate the energy at a point at the edge of the free
            surface at <m>z = h(t)</m> and the fluid leaving the hole at <m>z = 0</m>. At the free
            surface, the pressure is atmospheric, <m>p = p_\text{atm}</m>, and the speed is <m>
            u_S\sqrt{1+a^{'2}}</m>. At the hole, the pressure is also atmospheric, <m>p = p_\text{atm}</m>,
            and the velocity is <m>u</m>. Thus, Bernoulli's equation for the streamline joining the
            two points gives <me>
              \frac{p_\text{atm}}{\rho} + \frac12 u_S^2(1+a^{'2}) + gh = \frac{p_\text{atm}}{\rho} + \frac12
            u^2.
            </me>
            Assuming that <m>u_S \ll u</m>, this simplifies to <me>
              gh = \frac12 u^2.
            </me>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> Use the principle of conservation of mass to link <m>u_S</m>, <m>u</m>, <m>a_h</m> and <m>
            a(h(t))</m>; thereby determine the correct shape for a clepsydra. </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p> The volume flux out of the hole is <m>\pi a_h^2 u</m>, while the rate of decrease of
            the volume of fluid in the jar is <m>\pi a(h(t))^2 u_S</m>. Conservation of mass
            therefore gives <me>
              \pi a^2 u_S = \pi a_h^2 u,
            </me> or <me>
              u_S = \frac{a_h^2}{a^2}u.
            </me> Using the result from part
            (b) to eliminate <m>u</m> gives <me>
              u_S = \frac{a_h^2}{a^2}\sqrt{2gh}.
            </me> We require <m>u_S</m>
            to be independent of time so that equally spaced intervals are traversed in equal times.
            Hence, <me>
              a=\left(\frac{a_h^2\sqrt{2g}}{u_S}\right)^{1/2} h^{1/4}.
            </me>
            The terms in the brackets are constant, so we have that the radius of the jar must vary
            as the fourth root of the height. </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-rtt-stretching-cube">
      <title>Verifying Reynolds' Transport Theorem</title>
      <introduction>
        <aside>
          <title>New in 2026</title>
          <p>This exercise is a new addition to the notes in 2026.</p>
        </aside>

        <p> This exercise completes <xref ref="ex-rtt-stretching-cube-example" />. Consider the
          steady velocity field <m>\bu = \left(x, \; -\tfrac12 y, \; 0\right)</m>, and let <m>
          V(t)</m> be the material volume that initially occupies the unit cube <m>V(0) =
          [0,1]^3</m>. From the example, <m>V(t) = [0, \, \e^{t}] \times [0, \, \e^{-t/2}] \times
          [0, \, 1]</m> and the Jacobian is <m>J = \e^{t/2}</m>. Let <me>
            f(\bx) = x^2 + y, \qquad I(t) = \iiint_{V(t)} f \, \de{x} \, \de{y} \, \de{z}.
          </me>
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> By integrating directly over <m>V(t)</m>, compute <m>I(t)</m> and hence <m>
            \de{I}/\de{t}</m>. </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p> Integrating over <m>V(t)</m>, <md>
              <mrow>I(t) &amp;= \int_0^1 \int_0^{\e^{-t/2}} \int_0^{\e^{t}} (x^2 + y) \, \de{x}
            \, \de{y} \, \de{z}</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;= \underbrace{\frac{\e^{3t}}{3} \cdot \e^{-t/2}}_{x^2 \text{ term}}
                + \underbrace{\e^{t} \cdot \frac{\e^{-t}}{2}}_{y \text{ term}}
                = \frac13 \e^{5t/2} + \frac12.</mrow>
            </md> Therefore <me>
              \dd{I}{t} = \frac56 \e^{5t/2}.
            </me>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> Evaluate the right-hand side of Reynolds' Transport Theorem <xref ref="eqn-RTT" />
            and confirm that it agrees with your answer to the previous part. </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p> Here <m>\pd{f}{t} = 0</m> and <me>
              f\bu = \left( (x^2 + y)x, \; -\tfrac12 (x^2 + y)y, \; 0 \right),
            </me> so <me>
              \nabla \cdot (f\bu) = (3x^2 + y) - \tfrac12 (x^2 + 2y) = \tfrac52 x^2.
            </me> Hence <md>
              <mrow>\iiint_{V(t)} \left[ \pd{f}{t} + \nabla \cdot (f\bu) \right] \de{x} \,
            \de{y} \, \de{z}
                &amp;= \frac52 \int_0^1 \int_0^{\e^{-t/2}} \int_0^{\e^{t}} x^2 \, \de{x} \,
            \de{y} \, \de{z}</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;= \frac52 \cdot \frac{\e^{3t}}{3} \cdot \e^{-t/2} = \frac56
            \e^{5t/2},</mrow>
            </md> in agreement with the previous part. </p>

          <p> As a check, we can also follow the Lagrangian route used in the proof of <xref
              ref="thm-RTT" />. Mapping back to the fixed cube <m>V(0)</m>, <md>
              <mrow>I(t) &amp;= \iiint_{V(0)} f\big(\bx(\bX, t)\big) \, J \, \de{X} \, \de{Y} \,
            \de{Z}</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;= \int_0^1 \int_0^1 \int_0^1 \left( X^2 \e^{2t} + Y \e^{-t/2} \right)
            \e^{t/2} \, \de{X} \, \de{Y} \, \de{Z}</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;= \frac13 \e^{5t/2} + \frac12,</mrow>
            </md> which is now an integral over a <em>fixed</em> domain, so the time derivative
            can be taken inside the integral. </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-bernoulli-pitot">
      <title>Measuring airspeed with a Pitot tube</title>
      <introduction>
        <aside>
          <title>New in 2026</title>
          <p>This exercise is a new addition to the notes in 2026. It is optional and not
            part of the homework.</p>
        </aside>

        <p> Aircraft measure their airspeed using a <term>Pitot tube</term>: a thin tube that
          points forwards into the oncoming air, with its open tip facing the flow. The pressure
          inside the tube is compared with the pressure of the surrounding air. In the simplest
          version of the device, shown below, this comparison is made with a U-tube of water (a
          <term>manometer</term>). One arm of the U-tube is connected to the Pitot tube, and the
          other arm is open to the surrounding air. </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-pitot-cornell">
          <caption>A Pitot tube connected to a U-tube of water. <url
              href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DcSUjLyqYHI">Video</url> &#169; 2008
            Cornell University.</caption>
          <image source="ch-chapter03-pitot-cornell.jpeg" width="60%">
            <shortdescription>A hand holding a small tube connected by rubber tubing to one arm
              of a U-tube containing red liquid, whose two levels differ.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p> Watch <url
            href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DcSUjLyqYHI">this short video demonstration from
          Cornell University</url>. </p>

        <p> Suppose that air of constant density <m>\rho_\text{air}</m> approaches the tip of the
          tube with uniform speed <m>U</m> and pressure <m>p_\infty</m>. The flow is steady, and
          the air entering the tube pushes the water down in the arm connected to the tube and up
          in the open arm. Measured from the same reference level, let the water surface be at
          height <m>h_1</m> in the arm connected to the tube and at height <m>h_2</m> in the open
          arm, so that the height difference is <m>\Delta h = h_2 - h_1 \gt 0</m>. The water has
          density <m>\rho_w</m>. </p>
      </introduction>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> Explain why the air is brought to rest at the open tip of the tube. Hence, use an
            appropriate form of Bernoulli's equation to show that the pressure at the tip is <me>
              p_0 = p_\infty + \frac12 \rho_\text{air} U^2.
            </me>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <hint>
          <p> Follow the streamline that ends at the tip of the tube, where the air is brought to
            rest. Then treat the water in the U-tube as a fluid at rest, as in <xref
              ref="ex-bernoulli-hydrostatics" />. </p>
        </hint>

        <solution>
          <p> The tube is sealed at its far end by the water in the manometer, so once the water
            has settled, no air flows into the tube. The air arriving at the tip must therefore
            come to rest there: the tip is a <term>stagnation point</term>. </p>

          <p> The flow is steady and inviscid, so <xref ref="thm-bernoulli-steady" /> applies
            along the streamline that runs from far upstream to the tip. Far upstream the pressure
            is <m>p_\infty</m> and the speed is <m>U</m>; at the tip the pressure is <m>p_0</m> and
            the speed is zero. Changes in height along the streamline are small, so the gravity
            terms cancel. Hence <me>
              \frac{p_\infty}{\rho_\text{air}} + \frac12 U^2 = \frac{p_0}{\rho_\text{air}},
            </me> which gives <me>
              p_0 = p_\infty + \frac12 \rho_\text{air} U^2.
            </me> The pressure <m>p_0</m> is called the <term>stagnation pressure</term>. </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> The air inside the tube is at rest, so the pressure <m>p_0</m> is transmitted to the
            water in one arm of the U-tube. The other arm is open to air at pressure <m>
            p_\infty</m>. Neglecting the weight of the air in the tubes, show that <me>
              p_0 - p_\infty = \rho_w g \Delta h.
            </me>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p> The water is at rest, so from <xref ref="ex-bernoulli-hydrostatics" /> the quantity
            <m>p + \rho_w g z</m> is the same everywhere in the water. The water surface in the
            arm connected to the Pitot tube is at <m>z = h_1</m>, where the pressure is <m>p_0</m>,
            and the surface in the open arm is at <m>z = h_2</m>, where the pressure is <m>
            p_\infty</m>. Then <me>
              p_0 + \rho_w g h_1 = p_\infty + \rho_w g h_2,
            </me> so that <m>p_0 - p_\infty = \rho_w g (h_2 - h_1) = \rho_w g \Delta h</m>. </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p> Deduce that <me>
              U = \sqrt{\frac{2 \rho_w g \Delta h}{\rho_\text{air}}}.
            </me> Evaluate <m>U</m> when the water levels are at <m>h_1 = 0.25</m> m and <m>h_2 =
            0.75</m> m, taking <m>\rho_\text{air} = 1\,\text{kg/m}^3</m>,
            <m>\rho_w = 1000\,\text{kg/m}^3</m>, and <m>g = 10\,\text{m/s}^2</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p> Combining the two previous parts, <me>
              \frac12 \rho_\text{air} U^2 = p_0 - p_\infty = \rho_w g \Delta h,
            </me> and so <me>
              U = \sqrt{\frac{2 \rho_w g \Delta h}{\rho_\text{air}}}.
            </me> With the given values, <m>\Delta h = 0.75 - 0.25 = 0.5</m> m, so <me>
              p_0 - p_\infty = 1000 \times 10 \times 0.5 = 5000 \text{ Pa}, \qquad U^2 =
              \frac{2 \times 5000}{1} = 10^4 \text{ m}^2/\text{s}^2,
            </me> so <m>U = 100</m> m/s, or <m>360</m> km/h. This is roughly the take-off speed of
            a passenger jet. </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
    </exercise>

  </exercises>

</chapter>
